<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="RSM5" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>How Reliable is the Sample Mean?</title>

<p> 
    When we calculate the mean from a sample of fish, we need to ask: <term>how trustworthy is that number?</term> 
    Two key factors affect its reliability:
</p>
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Sample Size (n):</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            The number of individuals in your sample plays a major role. 
            If fish in the lake vary widely in size, a mean based on a small sample—say, 10 fish—might be misleading. 
            By chance, you could catch mostly larger or smaller individuals, skewing your estimate of the population average. 
            But if your sample includes 1,000 fish, it’s much more likely that the calculated mean will closely reflect the true population mean.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <term>Population Variation:</term>
        </p>
        <p>
            The required sample size depends on how much variation exists in the population. 
            In a fish farm where all fish are nearly the same size, even a small sample can yield reliable results. 
            But in a natural population with a wide range of sizes, a larger sample is needed to capture that diversity. 
            In short: <term>the more variable the population, the larger the sample must be to produce a reliable mean</term>.
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
<p>
    This leads to a core principle in data collection: the larger the sample, the lower the chance of random sampling error.
</p>
<p>
    We use n to represent the sample size and N for the total population size. 
    While N is often unknown, it can sometimes be estimated.
</p>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-RSM5-1">
    <title>Measuring Variation: Standard Deviation</title>
    <p>
        To quantify variation around the mean, we use a statistic called the <term>standard deviation (s.d.)</term>. 
        It tells us how spread out the measurements are. 
        We use:
    </p>
    <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            s for the standard deviation of a sample
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\sigma</m> (sigma) for the standard deviation of the entire population
        </p>
    </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Standard deviation is expressed in the same units as the original data—in this case, centimeters. 
        On a number line, it can be visualized as a portion of the range. 
        In a <term>normally distributed</term> population, about <term>95% of individuals fall within 1.96 standard deviations</term> of the mean. 
        This means that if you mark two standard deviations above and below the mean on a histogram’s x-axis, most of your data will fall within that span.
    </p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-RSM5-2">
    <title>Calculating Standard Deviation: Start with Variance</title>
    <p>
        To calculate standard deviation, we first compute a related statistic: <term>variance</term>. 
        Variance is the square of the standard deviation:
    </p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>s^2</m> = sample variance
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\sigma^2</m> = population variance
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
    <p>
        Variance is calculated by:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                Finding the difference between each observation and the mean.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Square the difference.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Averaging those squared values.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        Since variance involves squaring the units, it’s expressed in squared units (e.g., cm<m>^2</m>). 
        To return to the original units, we take the <term>square root of the variance</term>, which gives us the standard deviation in centimeters.
    </p>
</subsection>



</section>